00:07:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see the logic you're applying, and it's worth addressing directly because it sounds so sensible. You're saying: if, on average, gay men are more interested in fashion than straight men, then when I meet a gay man, it's perfectly reasonable to assume he's probably into fashion. That's how averages work. But here's the subtle problem: averages don't actually tell you what "most" individuals are like. An average can go up because a smaller number of people are extremely into something, while the majority are not. Think of wealth: the average wealth in a room can be millions if one billionaire walks in, but "most" people in that room are not millionaires. If fashion interest worked the same way — a highly visible, influential minority of very fashion-forward gay men pulling up the average while many others aren't that bothered — then your "reasonable expectation" would be wrong for most of the gay men you meet. And even if it *were* true of most — say, 60% — that still leaves 40% who don't fit. When you're looking at a specific person, you have no idea if you're looking at the 60% or the 40%. The only way to know is to let the person show you, not to assume. That's the gap. I'm not saying the average doesn't exist in some form. I'm saying moving from an average to an assumption about a stranger is a real-world error, not just a political nicety. It's how you end up assigning styling work to someone who has no interest and no skill, because you mistook the group tendency for a personal guarantee.
